* Three Clocks: Period, Release, Knowledge Time
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Compustat.2017.txt:25 '* Effective date'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Compustat.2017.txt:34 '* Observation date'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:1886 '#### Date and period references'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:562 '* Fundamental data: release data vs report data'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=58%}
- @Definition@: a fundamental record has three clocks
  - **Period end** (`datadate`): what the numbers describe
  - **Release** (`rdq`): when the company publishes
  - **Knowledge time** (`effdate`): when the database holds the value

- @Problem@: the release comes weeks or months after the period end
  - Papers that join on the period end cannot be reproduced once the data is
    aligned

- @Key idea@: a value is usable at decision time $t$ only if its knowledge
  time is not after $t$
::::
:::: {.column width=42%}

```graphviz
digraph Clocks {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.3;
    ranksep=0.3;
    rankdir=TB;
    // period : orange | release : teal | database : blue
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=11, margin="0.18,0.08", height=0.42];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    Period  [label="Period end\n(datadate)", fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    Release [label="Release\n(rdq): +25 to 181 days", fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Load    [label="Database load\n(effdate)", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    Period -> Release -> Load;
}
```
::::
:::

* A Point-in-Time Data Model
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Compustat.2017.txt:16 '- Snapshot DB shows information that was available at that time in history'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:1845 '#### Overview'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:580 '* Point-in-time'

- @Definition@: a **point-in-time database** stores several values of each
  variable, each with the interval during which it was the known value
  - A record is `(asset, period, value, from, through)`
  - A query on an observation date returns the records with `from` $\le$ date
    $<$ `through`

- @Example@: one restated record of the running example

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Stock", "Period end", "Value", "From", "Through"),
  rows: (
    ("S0272", "2012-09-30", "-0.07", "2012-11-25", "2012-12-21"),
    ("S0272", "2012-09-30", "-1.56", "2012-12-21", "9999-12-31"),
  ),
  bold-first-col: false,
)
```
\endgroup

- @Remark@: the restatement does not erase the first row
  - A query on 2012-12-01 returns -0.07, and one on 2013-01-15 returns -1.56

* As-of Joins
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1845 '* Look-ahead bias'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=45%}
- @Definition@: an **as-of join** attaches to each decision time $t$ the latest
  record of the asset with knowledge time $\tau \le t$
  $$
  x_i(t) \triangleq v_{i,k^*}
  \quad
  k^* \triangleq \arg\max_k \{ \tau_{i,k} : \tau_{i,k} \le t \}
  $$

- @Key idea@: the only change is the join key, `datadate` $\to$ `rdq`
  - The period end join uses an unreleased value on 5 of 8 decisions

```python
pd.merge_asof(
    decisions, funds,
    left_on="decision_date",
    right_on="rdq",
    by="stock",
)
```
::::
:::: {.column width=55%}

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Decision", "Period end join", "Release join"),
  rows: (
    ("2024-01-31", "4.1 (future)", "none"),
    ("2024-02-29", "4.1", "4.1"),
    ("2024-03-31", "4.6 (future)", "4.1"),
    ("2024-04-30", "4.6 (future)", "4.1"),
    ("2024-05-31", "4.6", "4.6"),
    ("2024-06-30", "5.2 (future)", "4.6"),
    ("2024-07-31", "5.2 (future)", "4.6"),
    ("2024-08-31", "5.2", "5.2"),
  ),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup
::::
:::

* Release Lags and Look-Ahead
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:562 '* Fundamental data: release data vs report data'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Fact@: the simulated lags have a mean of 59 days, from 25 to 181

- @Problem@: a fixed lag after the quarter end leaks the earnings news
  - Lag of 0 to 30 days: IC 0.089, three times the 0.029 of the true release
    dates
  - Lag of 45 days: 0.053, and of 90 days: 0.032

- @Key idea@: store the release timestamp of every record
  - A lag rule must exceed the largest lag
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

![](book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/lectures_source/figures/L04.01.Release_Lag.png)

\footnotesize _Distribution of release lags (top), and mean IC against the
assumed lag, with the IC of the true release dates as a dashed line (bottom)_
::::
:::

* Restatements, Revisions, and Backfill
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Compustat.2017.txt:110 '* Restatement'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Compustat.2017.txt:113 '* Material change / error'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:570 '* Fundamental data: backfilling'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:573 '* Fundamental data: reinstated value'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: a **restatement** revises a past statement to correct a
  material error, e.g., GDP is revised twice after its release

- @Definition@: **backfilling** fills history with data unknown at the
  publication date

- @Comparison@: three ways to read a restated value
  - _Latest vintage_: IC 0.044
  - _Point in time_: IC 0.027
  - _First reported_: IC 0.029

- @Key idea@: the latest vintage knows the restatement before it happens
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

![](book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/lectures_source/figures/L04.01.Restatements.png)

\footnotesize _Vintages of one restated record (top), and mean IC under three
rules to read it (bottom)_
::::
:::

* Pipelines That Fail Loudly
// From: data605/lectures_source/Lesson02.3-Data_Pipelines.smd:373 '* Data Cleaning'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=58%}
- @Problem@: a point-in-time bug raises no error
  - The join runs, the metric looks good, and the future sits in the features

- @Solution@: make the knowledge time a checked column
  - Every join asserts `knowledge_time` $\le$ `decision_time`
  - Records dated after the clock of the run fail the load
  - Coding assistants generate one guard and test per join

- @Example@: the guard finds 5 future rows of 8 in the period end join, and 0
  in the as-of join
::::
:::: {.column width=42%}

```graphviz
digraph Guards {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.3;
    ranksep=0.3;
    rankdir=TB;
    // input : orange | guarded step : teal | output : blue
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=11, margin="0.18,0.08", height=0.42];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    Ingest [label="Ingest\nguard: no future dates", fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Table  [label="Point-in-time table", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Join   [label="As-of join\nguard: known before\ndecision", fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];

    Ingest -> Table -> Join;
}
```
::::
