# Survivorship Bias

* Point-in-Time Universes
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:130 '* Does data suffer from survivorship bias?'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:237 '* Dealing with survivorship biased data'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1841 '* Survivorship bias'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:2587 '- Eliminate stocks priced < $1'

- @Definition@: a **survivorship-bias-free universe** at date $t$ contains
  every asset that was listed and tradable at $t$
  - It includes the assets that later went bankrupt, merged, or delisted
  - Such data is sometimes called "point-in-time"

- @Problem@: using the current universe, e.g., today's index members, for the
  past
  - Databases free from survivorship bias are expensive

- @Solution@: select the assets with `listed_on` $\le t <$ `delisted_on`, then
  apply rules with data known at $t$
  - E.g., price above \$1 and market capitalization at $t$
  - Or collect the data yourself, so that it is point-in-time

* Delistings: Causes and Returns
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:130 '* Does data suffer from survivorship bias?'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:6657 '  - Delisting info to handle survivorship bias'

- @Definition@: a **delisting** removes a security from an exchange, and the
  database must record the reason

// TODO(gp): Check the table: the reasons and last returns come from general
// knowledge and are not in the notes
\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Reason", "Last return"),
  rows: (
    ("Merger or acquisition", "Close to the deal price"),
    ("Bankruptcy or liquidation", "Large loss, up to -100%"),
    ("Failure to meet listing rules", "Large loss"),
  ),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup

- @Fact@: delistings for negative reasons are generally surprises, and the
  correct delisting return is not available for most of them in CRSP
  (Shumway, 1997)

// TODO(gp): Check the -30% mean: it is an assumption of the simulation, set
// by the author after recalling the size of the omitted returns, and it is not
// in the abstract of Shumway (1997)
- @Example@: in the simulation 36% of the stocks delist, with a mean delisting
  return of -30%
  - 86% of the cheapest decile delists, against 4% of the most expensive

* Missing Delisting Returns
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1841 '* Survivorship bias'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:130 '* Does data suffer from survivorship bias?'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=52%}
- @Problem@: the delisting month of a failing stock often has no return, and
  the loss disappears from every average

- @Example@: the value premium, i.e., cheapest minus most expensive decile
  - True delisting return: 3.7% a year
  - Delisting return missing: 6.5%
  - Imputed return of -30%: 3.5%

- @Interpretation@: missing returns inflate the premium by 78%

- @Solution@: take the delisting return from the vendor, or impute a
  conservative one
::::
:::: {.column width=48%}

![](book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/lectures_source/figures/L04.01.Delisting_Returns.png)

\footnotesize _Returns of the delisting months (top), and annual return of the
cheapness deciles under three rules (bottom)_
::::
:::

* Data-Quality Tests for the Universe
// From: data605/lectures_source/Lesson02.3-Data_Pipelines.smd:373 '* Data Cleaning'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:237 '* Dealing with survivorship biased data'

- @Problem@: survivorship bias is a property of what is missing, so no check
  on a single value finds it

- @Techniques@: tests that run at every data refresh
  1. Every exit has a delisting date and a reason
  2. Every delisting has a delisting return, or an imputed flag
  3. The assets per date match an external count, e.g., index membership
  4. No asset has a price after its delisting date

- @Applications@: automation fails the load when a vendor drops the delisted
  names, and coding assistants generate the tests from the schema

- @Remark@: detecting a survivor-only panel from the data alone is covered in
  detail in Lesson 06.01

# Reference Data

* Corporate Actions: A Catalog
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:215 '* Are the data split / dividend adjusted?'

- @Definition@: a **corporate action** is an event decided by a company that
  changes its securities

// TODO(gp): Check the spin-off, merger, and name change rows: they come from
// general knowledge and are not in the notes
\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Action", "Raw price", "Adjustment of earlier data"),
  rows: (
    ("Split N for 1", "Divided by N", "Prices times 1/N, volume times N"),
    ("Reverse split", "Multiplied by N", "Prices times N, volume divided by N"),
    ("Cash dividend D", "Falls by about D", "Prices times (P-D)/P"),
    ("Spin-off", "Falls", "New security, factor for the old"),
    ("Merger", "Target ends", "Delisting return, link identifiers"),
    ("Name change", "Unchanged", "New label, same security"),
  ),
  col-widths: (1fr, 1fr, 1.8fr),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup

- @Fact@: without adjustments, the price jumps at the ex-date open compared
  with the previous close, besides the normal fluctuations

* Split Adjustment
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:219 '* Adjusting for stock splits'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:789 '* Volume bars: cons'

- @Definition@: in an $N$-to-1 split with ex-date $T$, each old share becomes
  $N$ shares: prices before $T$ are multiplied by $1/N$, and volume by $N$
  $$
  \tilde P_t \triangleq P_t \prod_{T > t} \frac{1}{N_T}
  $$
  - The product runs over all the splits after date $t$

- @Example@: the simulated stock splits 2 for 1 twice in 20 years
  - Its first raw close of \$61.98 is \$15.49 split-adjusted

- @Problem@: the raw return on a split day is wrong
  - The worst raw daily return is -49.0%, and the worst adjusted one is -5.1%

* Dividends and Total Returns
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:227 '* Adjusting for dividends'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:1321 '** Adjusting for dividends'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: the **total return** adds the dividend to the price return
  $$
  R^{tot}_t \triangleq R_t + \frac{D_t}{P_{t-1}}
  $$

- @Fact@: the price falls by about $D$ on the ex-date
  - Multiply earlier prices by $(P - D) / P$: the returns do not change
  - Adding $D$ to prices changes the returns and can make prices negative

- @Example@: with a 2% yearly yield, \$1 grows to \$7.7 with dividends and
  to \$5.2 without
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

![](book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/lectures_source/figures/L04.01.Adjusted_Prices.png)

\footnotesize _Growth of \$1 with dividends and with the price only (top), and
factor that turns the raw close into the adjusted close (bottom)_
::::
:::

* Store Raw, Adjust on Read
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:215 '* Are the data split / dividend adjusted?'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=58%}
- @Problem@: adjusted prices depend on the future, since every new split or
  dividend rescales the whole history

- @Example@: the simulated stock closed at \$97.84 on 2010-06-30
  - Today a database shows \$48.92 split-adjusted, and \$36.58 fully adjusted
    (factor 0.374)

- @Solution@: store raw prices and a table of corporate actions, and compute
  the factors on read for a chosen as-of date
::::
:::: {.column width=42%}

```graphviz
digraph Adjust {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.3;
    ranksep=0.3;
    rankdir=TB;
    // input : orange | process : teal | output : blue
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=11, margin="0.18,0.08", height=0.42];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    Raw    [label="Raw prices", fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    Action [label="Corporate-action\ntable", fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    Adjust [label="adjust(as_of)", fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Out    [label="Adjusted series\nas known at as_of", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    Raw -> Adjust;
    Action -> Adjust;
    Adjust -> Out;
}
```
::::
:::

* Tickers Are Not Keys
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.bloomberg.txt:90 '## Securities'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=58%}
- @Problem@: a ticker is a label that an exchange assigns and reassigns
  - One company can have several tickers over time, and one ticker several
    companies

// TODO(gp): Check both examples: they come from memory and are not in the
// notes, in particular the rename date of Meta and the 2005 deal of SBC
- @Examples@: two directions of the problem
  - **Meta Platforms** traded as `FB` before its rename and as `META` after
  - The ticker `T` went from **AT&T Corp.** to the new AT&T Inc., after SBC
    Communications bought it in 2005

- @Key idea@: join on a permanent identifier, and keep the ticker as an
  attribute with a date range
::::
:::: {.column width=42%}

```graphviz
