* Security Master and Time-Varying Links
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:1815 '### CRSP'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=52%}
- @Definition@: a **security master** is the reference table of every
  security and its identifiers over time

- @Problem@: a link is true only for a date range
  - A join on the wrong date attaches the numbers of one company to the
    returns of another

- @Key idea@: identity is a time-varying fact, so it needs a knowledge time
  and an as-of join like any other value
::::
:::: {.column width=48%}

```graphviz
digraph Master {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.3;
    ranksep=0.35;
    rankdir=TB;
    // master : blue | dated mappings : teal
    node [shape=record, style="filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=10, margin="0.12,0.06"];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75];

    Sec  [label="{security|security_id\lissuer_id\lshare_class\llisted_on\ldelisted_on\l}", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Ident [label="{identifier|security_id\ltype\lvalue\lvalid_from\lvalid_to\l}", fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Link [label="{vendor_link|security_id\lvendor\lvendor_id\llink_from\llink_to\l}", fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];

    Sec -> Ident;
    Sec -> Link;
}
```
::::
:::

* Event, Exchange, and Receipt Time
// From: data605/lectures_source/Lesson10.2-Streaming_and_Real_Time_Analytics.smd:183 '* Event vs Processing Time'
// From: data605/lectures_source/Lesson10.2-Streaming_and_Real_Time_Analytics.smd:161 '* Delivery Semantics: At-least once'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:1872 '#### Terminology'

- @Definition@: one market event has several times
  - **Event time**: when the event was generated at the venue
  - **Processing time**: when the system received it
  - **Knowledge time**: when the vendor database held the value

- @Problem@: events arrive late, out of order, or duplicated (at-least-once
  delivery)

- @Example@: Compustat effective times are weekly and intraday updates, which
  is not the same as its FTP delivery schedule

- @Key idea@: store UTC timestamps, keep the event time and the receipt time,
  and de-duplicate on the asset, the event time, and a sequence number

* Time Zones, Daylight Saving, and Calendars
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:271 '** NASDAQ trading market hours'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:276 '** Daylight saving time'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:290 '** Eastern Time'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Fact@: the US session runs from 9:30 to 16:00 ET, which is UTC-5 in winter
  and UTC-4 in summer

- @Example@: the 16:00 New York close is 21:00 UTC in winter and 20:00 UTC in
  summer
  - New York and London are 5 hours apart, except 28 days a year, since the
    clocks change on different dates

// TODO(gp): Check the calendar technique: it comes from memory and is not in
// the notes
- @Techniques@: store UTC and convert only at the edge
  - Use one exchange calendar per market, never the next weekday
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

![](book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/lectures_source/figures/L04.01.UTC_Close.png)

\footnotesize _UTC hour of the 16:00 New York close (top), and hours between New
York and London (bottom), in gray where the gap is not 5_
::::
:::

* Automating Reference Data
// From: data605/lectures_source/Lesson07.2-Data_Wrangling.smd:302 '* Multi-Source Problems'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:243 '* Does your strategy use high and low data?'

- @Problem@: reference data errors do not raise exceptions, and break joins
  silently

- @Solution@: ML and automation for three common failures
  - _Entity resolution_: map names and tickers across identifier systems
  - _Anomaly detection_: flag bad ticks and unadjusted splits, e.g., a return
    4 standard deviations away with no news
  - _Reconciliation_: every price jump matches a corporate-action row

// TODO(gp): Check the entity resolution steps: they are a generic recipe
// written from memory, and are not in the notes
- @Techniques@: entity resolution in four steps
  1. Normalize the names
  2. Block on a coarse key, e.g., the country
  3. Score each candidate pair with a string similarity or an embedding
  4. Send the uncertain band to a person

- @Remark@: detecting these errors in a panel of unknown quality is covered in
  detail in Lesson 06.01

# Data Vendors

* Four Types of Financial Data
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:532 '* Essential types of financial data'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:543 '* Fundamental data'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:594 '* Market data'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:626 '* Analytics'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:652 '* Alternative data'

- @Definition@: financial data comes in four types, in increasing diversity

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Type", "Examples", "Main pitfall"),
  rows: (
    ("Fundamental", "Assets, sales, earnings", "Release vs report date, backfill"),
    ("Market", "Price, volume, quotes", "Over 10 TB a day, outliers"),
    ("Analytics", "Ratings, recommendations, sentiment", "Costly, opaque, not exclusive"),
    ("Alternative", "Social media, satellites", "Cost, privacy, hard to process"),
  ),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup

- @Key idea@: start from the rawest data available, and do not use data
  processed by somebody else without knowing the processing

* CRSP: Prices, Returns, Delistings
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:1815 '### CRSP'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:6653 '- From CRSP / Compustat'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:6657 '  - Delisting info to handle survivorship bias'

- @Definition@: **CRSP** (Center for Research in Security Prices) is a database
  of US stock data
  - Daily and monthly returns, shares outstanding, and trading volumes
  - Delisting information, to handle survivorship bias

// TODO(gp): Check these CRSP characteristics: PERMNO and total returns come
// from memory, and are not in the notes
- @Characteristics@
  - Includes delisted stocks, and identifies each security with a permanent
    number, PERMNO
  - Gives returns that include dividends

- @Problem@: the delisting return is missing for most negative delistings
  - The omitted returns are large (Shumway, 1997)

* TAQ: Tick Data and Bars
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:6646 '- TAQ'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:7204 '- Trade and Quotes (TAQ) tick record'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:608 '* Market data: pros and cons'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:687 '* Bar data'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:751 '* Tick bars: outliers'

- @Definition@: **TAQ** (Trade and Quotes) holds the consolidated intraday
  transactions and quotes of all the securities on NYSE, AMEX, and NASDAQ

- @Problem@: raw market data is big and needs cleaning
  - It can exceed 10 TB a day, and an auction prints as one large tick

- @Definition@: **bars** turn irregular ticks into a regular table

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Bar", "Sampled when", "Weakness"),
  rows: (
    ("Time", "A fixed interval passes", "Oversamples quiet periods"),
    ("Tick", "N transactions trade", "Order splitting changes the count"),
    ("Volume", "N shares trade", "Shares per dollar change with price"),
    ("Dollar", "N dollars trade", "Size needs scaling by market cap"),
  ),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup

* Compustat: Fundamentals
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Compustat.2017.txt:5 '- Historical and backtesting database providing financial data on publicly traded'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:1826 '- Compustat data is organized around company / security (GVKEY) and issue'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:1886 '#### Date and period references'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:1899 '#### Data collection'

- @Definition@: **Compustat** provides standardized fundamentals of public
  companies: income statement, balance sheet, and cash flow
  - Annual and quarterly, preliminary and final, keyed by `GVKEY` and `IID`

- @Characteristics@: **Compustat Snapshot** is the point-in-time product,
  with the original data and every change since about 1986

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Field", "Meaning"),
  rows: (
    ("datadate", "Fiscal period end"),
    ("rdq", "Earnings release date"),
    ("effdate, thrudate", "Interval during which the value is known"),
    ("upd", "Status: limited, preliminary, or final"),
  ),
  col-widths: (1fr, 2.4fr),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup

- @Problem@: a copy that keeps only the latest restated values is not
  point-in-time

* EDGAR: Filings
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.edgar.txt:2 '# 2012, Decoding Inside Information'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.edgar.txt:111 '# 2018, Lazy prices'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.edgar.txt:166 '# 2018, Do hedge funds profit from public information?'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:6482 '### Form 8-K'

- @Definition@: **EDGAR** is the public archive of the filings that companies
  and insiders send to the SEC
  - _10-K_ and _10-Q_: annual and quarterly reports
  - _8-K_: unscheduled events, e.g., a bankruptcy or the departure of the CEO
  - _Form 4_: trades of insiders, and _Form 13_: holdings of institutions

- @Fact@: the filing delay is part of the data
  - Insiders report within 2 days since 2002, and the median delay between the
    trade and the report is 3 days

// TODO(gp): Check that the acceptance time stamped by the SEC on a filing is
// the best knowledge time: it is recalled from memory
- @Key idea@: the knowledge time of a filing is its public dissemination time,
  not the period that it describes

- @Applications@: signals from insider trades (Cohen, Malloy, and Pomorski,
  2012), 10-K language changes (Cohen, Malloy, and Nguyen, 2020), and filing
  acquisition by hedge funds (Crane, Crotty, and Umar, 2023)

* XBRL: Structured Facts
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.XBRL.txt:212 '* Extensible Business Reporting Language (XBRL)'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.XBRL.txt:222 '* XBRL instance'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.XBRL.txt:236 '* XBRL Contexts'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.XBRL.txt:239 '* XBRL taxonomy'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.XBRL.txt:245 '* iXBRL'

- @Definition@: **XBRL** (Extensible Business Reporting Language) is an open,
  XML-based standard in which each fact is tagged with its meaning and context
  - Inline XBRL embeds the tags in the HTML of the filing

- @Components@: an XBRL file
  - _Items_: facts with one value, and _tuples_: facts with several values
  - _Contexts_: the entity and the period of a fact
  - _Taxonomy_: the schemas that define the concepts

// TODO(gp): Check the challenges: they are a reasoned summary and are not in
// the notes
- @Challenges@: companies can extend the taxonomy, and a fact is as reported,
  so restatements and filing times need to be stored

- @Key idea@: use XBRL to fill the point-in-time table, with one row per fact
  and the filing time as knowledge time

* Vendor Adapters and One Schema
// From: data605/lectures_source/Lesson02.3-Data_Pipelines.smd:223 '* ETL Paradigm: Example'
// From: data605/lectures_source/Lesson02.3-Data_Pipelines.smd:267 '* ELT Paradigm'
// From: data605/lectures_source/Lesson07.3-Serialization_Formats.smd:85 '* (Apache) Parquet'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:237 '* Dealing with survivorship biased data'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Problem@: vendors differ in format, identifiers, time zones, and quality

- @Solution@: one **adapter** per vendor maps the raw files to one schema
  - Keep raw files immutable, and store clean tables in Parquet
  - Snapshot each file with its retrieval time, to build a point-in-time
    history

- @Applications@: AI assistants draft the adapter and its contract tests from
  the vendor documentation, and a person reviews them
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

```graphviz
digraph Adapters {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.3;
    ranksep=0.3;
    rankdir=TB;
    // source : orange | process : teal | output : blue
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=11, margin="0.18,0.08", height=0.42];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    Raw      [label="Raw vendor files\n(immutable)", fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    Adapters [label="One adapter\nper vendor", fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Canon    [label="Canonical\npoint-in-time tables", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    Raw -> Adapters -> Canon;
}
```
::::
:::

# Alternative Data

* Classifying Alternative Data
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:652 '* Alternative data'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:656 '* Alternative data: examples'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:5604 '* Data generated by individuals'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:5617 '* Data generated by business processes'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:5623 '* Data generated by sensors'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:5722 '* Attributes of alt-data'

- @Definition@: **alternative data** is everything that is not fundamental
  data, market data, or analytics

- @Classification@: by the source of the data
  - _Individuals_: social media, news, web searches, reviews
  - _Business processes_: transactions, credit cards, scanner data
  - _Sensors_: satellites, geolocation, weather, Internet of Things

- @Characteristics@: attributes to record for every dataset

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Attribute", "Question"),
  rows: (
    ("How well known", "Public, limited sales, or exclusive?"),
    ("Processing", "Raw, processed, signal, or report?"),
    ("Quality", "History, gaps, backfill, method?"),
    ("Alpha content", "Net of cost, orthogonal, capacity?"),
  ),
  col-widths: (1fr, 2fr),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup

* Satellite Data
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:5379 '#### Orbital Insight (OI)'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:5388 '##### Oil tank data for crude inventories forecast'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:5396 '##### Carpark data for Macy's sales forecast'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=58%}
- @Examples@: counts from images become indicators
  - **Oil tank counts** forecast crude inventories: weekly data from 2010 to
    2017, over 5000 tanks
  - **Car park counts** lead the same-store sales of Macy's

- @Challenges@
  - Clouds block the view
  - Car parks cover stores, not e-commerce

- @Key idea@: the knowledge time is the delivery of the processed number, not
  the time of the image
::::
:::: {.column width=42%}

```graphviz
digraph Satellite {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.3;
    ranksep=0.3;
    rankdir=TB;
    // source : orange | process : teal | output : blue
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=11, margin="0.18,0.08", height=0.42];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    Image  [label="Image", fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    Counts [label="Counts, corrected\nfor seasonality", fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Signal [label="Signal\nknown at delivery", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    Image -> Counts -> Signal;
}
```
::::
:::

* Web Data
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:5284 '* Google trends'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:5530 '- No need to create / maintain scraping / crawler'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:5896 '* Big data compliance'

- @Examples@: web-crawled prices, reviews and ratings, and online search
  - **Google Trends** shows how often a term is searched, compared with the
    total search volume

- @Characteristics@: Google Trends
  - Pros: high frequency, 10 years of history, and objective, since it is not
    a survey
  - Cons: ambiguity (Apple vs. apple), seasonality, and outliers

- @Challenges@: every web site is different, so a scraper needs maintenance
  - Scraping might face legal challenges, and data can have terms of use

// TODO(gp): Check that the past of a web page is usually unrecoverable: it is a
// reasoned claim, and archives may hold some pages
- @Key idea@: the web has no point-in-time history, so store each page with its
  retrieval time

* ESG and Climate Risk Data
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:5536 '## EagleAlpha'

// TODO(gp): Check this whole slide: ESG and climate risk data are not covered
// in the notes, and the content is written from memory except the ratings
// divergence of Berg, Koelbel, and Rigobon (2022)
- @Definition@: **ESG data** measure the environmental, social, and governance
  practices of a company, from its disclosures and from rating providers
  - **Climate risk data** measure its exposure to physical risk, e.g., floods,
    and to transition risk, e.g., carbon prices

- @Fact@: the ratings of the same firm diverge across six providers
  - Measurement explains 56% of the divergence, scope 38%, and weights 6%
    (Berg, Koelbel, and Rigobon, 2022)

- @Problem@: the history is short and not point-in-time
  - Disclosures are voluntary, and providers rewrite past scores

- @Key idea@: treat an ESG score as the output of a vendor model, with a
  version and a knowledge time, not as a measurement

* Pitfalls and Evaluation
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:5658 '* Setbacks from alt-data research'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:5782 '* Quality'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:5971 '* Short history and seasonality'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:5896 '* Big data compliance'

- @Problem@: many alternative datasets fail
  - No alpha, low capacity, fast decay, high price, or spurious relationships

- @Example@: a short history hides seasonality
  - Lower Chipotle foot traffic was read as food poisoning, but people were
    ordering from home in the cold season

- @Techniques@: a checklist before buying

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Check", "Question"),
  rows: (
    ("Point in time", "Are knowledge timestamps kept, with no backfill?"),
    ("History", "Does it cover seasons and regimes?"),
    ("Orthogonality", "Does it add to the known alpha sources?"),
    ("Economics", "Does alpha net of cost justify the price?"),
    ("Compliance", "Are terms of use and personal data clear?"),
  ),
  col-widths: (1fr, 2.4fr),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup

* LLM Extraction of Unstructured Sources
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:5852 '* Big data workflow for investment managers'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:679 '* Alternative data: cons'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Problem@: web pages, filings, and PDFs have no fixed schema, so rule-based
  parsers break often

// TODO(gp): Check these steps: they are a design written from experience, and
// there is no source in the notes
- @Solution@: an LLM extraction model turns each document into structured,
  timestamped fields
  1. Define the output schema
  2. Prompt the model with the text and the schema
  3. Validate: parts add up, the value is in the text
  4. Store the field with `published_at` and `extracted_at`

- @Key idea@: the knowledge time is `published_at`, when the document became
  public, not `extracted_at`
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

```graphviz
digraph Extract {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.3;
    ranksep=0.3;
    rankdir=TB;
    // source : orange | process : teal | output : blue | review : coral
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=11, margin="0.18,0.08", height=0.42];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    Doc   [label="Filing, web page,\nPDF", fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    LLM   [label="LLM extractor", fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Check [label="Validator", fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Table [label="Point-in-time table", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Human [label="Human review", fillcolor="#F6C6C6", color="#D98C8C", fontcolor="#6B2A2A"];

    Doc -> LLM -> Check -> Table;
    Check -> Human [label="  fails  "];
}
```
::::
:::

* Summary

- @Key idea@: data engineering makes every value what a trader could have
  known, with the right time, assets, units, and entity

- @Components@: correctness and survivorship
  - Store the knowledge time, join as-of, keep every vintage, and assert it
  - Build universes with the delisted assets, and keep delisting returns

- @Components@: reference data and vendors
  - Store raw prices and corporate actions, and adjust on read
  - Join on permanent identifiers and UTC, and put each vendor behind one
    adapter

- @Components@: alternative data
  - Check history, point-in-time availability, cost, and compliance first
  - Use LLMs to extract structure, and validate every field

